  "content": "You're designing a part for someone to 3D print at home. They'll print it on their own printer, use only the hardware listed below, and put it together by following your manual.\n\n## Their setup\n- Printer: Bambu Lab X1C (with AMS), build volume 256 x 256 x 256 mm\n- Nozzle: 0.4 mm\n- Layer height: 0.2 mm\n- Filament: PLA (high-speed PLA, black)\n- Longest bridge seen to print cleanly on this setup: 50 mm\n\n## Hardware they can use\nRefer to an item by its catalog reference: \"ISO 4762 M5x30\" (socket head screw, size x length, from the lengths listed), \"ISO 4032 M5\" (hex nut), \"ISO 10511 M5\" (nylon-insert lock nut), \"ISO 7089 M5\" (washer), \"ASME B18.2.2 1/4-20\" (1/4\"-20 nut), \"insert M3x4x5\" (heat-set insert), \"salvaged:webcam-mount-bolt\".\n\n```yaml\npitch_mm:\n  M3: 0.5\n  M4: 0.7\n  M5: 0.8\n  M6: 1.0\n  M8: 1.25\n  1/4-20: 1.27\nscrews:\n- family: socket head cap screw\n  standard: ISO 4762\n  head: cylindrical\n  sizes:\n    M3:\n      d: 3\n      head_d: 5.5\n      head_h: 3\n      b: 18\n      threaded_to_head_max: 20\n    M4:\n      d: 4\n      head_d: 7.0\n      head_h: 4\n      b: 20\n      threaded_to_head_max: 25\n    M5:\n      d: 5\n      head_d: 8.5\n      head_h: 5\n      b: 22\n      threaded_to_head_max: 25\n    M6:\n      d: 6\n      head_d: 10.0\n      head_h: 6\n      b: 24\n      threaded_to_head_max: 30\n    M8:\n      d: 8\n      head_d: 13.0\n      head_h: 8\n      b: 28\n      threaded_to_head_max: 35\n  lengths_mm:\n  - 6\n  - 8\n  - 10\n  - 12\n  - 16\n  - 20\n  - 25\n  - 30\n  - 35\n  - 40\n  - 45\n  - 50\nnuts:\n- family: hex nut\n  standard: ISO 4032\n  sizes:\n    M3:\n      af: 5.5\n      h: 2.4\n    M4:\n      af: 7.0\n      h: 3.2\n    M5:\n      af: 8.0\n      h: 4.7\n    M6:\n      af: 10.0\n      h: 5.2\n    M8:\n      af: 13.0\n      h: 6.8\n- family: nylon-insert lock nut\n  standard: ISO 10511\n  sizes:\n    M3:\n      af: 5.5\n      h: 4.0\n    M4:\n      af: 7.0\n      h: 5.0\n    M5:\n      af: 8.0\n      h: 5.0\n    M6:\n      af: 10.0\n      h: 6.0\n    M8:\n      af: 13.0\n      h: 8.0\n- family: hex nut\n  standard: ASME B18.2.2 (finished hex)\n  sizes:\n    1/4-20:\n      af: 11.11\n      h: 5.56\n      af_measured: 10.92\nwashers:\n- family: plain washer\n  standard: ISO 7089\n  sizes:\n    M3:\n      id: 3.2\n      od: 7\n      t: 0.5\n    M4:\n      id: 4.3\n      od: 9\n      t: 0.8\n    M5:\n      id: 5.3\n      od: 10\n      t: 1.0\n    M6:\n      id: 6.4\n      od: 12\n      t: 1.6\n    M8:\n      id: 8.4\n      od: 16\n      t: 1.6\ninserts:\n- family: heat-set threaded insert\n  standard: none (vendor-specific)\n  sizes:\n    M3x4x5:\n      thread: M3\n      length: 4\n      od: 5\n      hole_d: 4.3\nsalvaged:\n- id: webcam-mount-bolt\n  description: 1/4\"-20 bolt from a webcam monitor mount\n  thread: 1/4-20\n  head_d: null\n  head_h: null\n  length: null\nservos:\n- id: MG90S\n  body_l:\n    measured:\n    - 22.0\n    - 22.63\n    note: two readings of the length; 22.63 used\n  body_w:\n    measured: 12.33\n  body_h:\n    measured: 22.5\n    note: '''top to bottom''; not used by the coupon'\n  spline_od:\n    measured: 4.57\n  tab_hole_d:\n    measured: 2.17\n  tab_top_to_case_top:\n    measured: 1.67\n    note: from the tab's top face (same method as MG996R)\n  tab_t:\n    measured: 2.55\n  tab_face_to_case_top:\n    used: 4.22\n    note: tab top to case top + tab thickness (1.67 + 2.55)\n  tab_hole_pitch:\n    measured: 25.84\n    used: 28.01\n    note: caliper tips closed into the two holes, so inner edges; centres = 25.84\n      + 2.17. Datasheet ~27.8\n- id: MG996R\n  body_l:\n    measured: 40.58\n  body_w:\n    measured: 19.82\n  body_h:\n    measured:\n    - 40.41\n    - 40.4\n    note: '''front to back'''\n  output_top_d:\n    measured: 5.6\n    note: '''the thing at the top''; unclear which feature'\n  spline_od:\n    measured: 5.84\n    note: '''if I grab here'''\n  tab_hole_d:\n    measured: 4.58\n  tab_hole_pitch:\n    measured: 44.61\n    used: 49.19\n    note: inner edges as for MG90S; centres = 44.61 + 4.58. Datasheet ~49.5. Spacing\n      across each tab's hole pair not measured (datasheet 10)\n  tab_shape:\n    note: each tab has two holes open to the edge (U-slots) and a stiffening rib on\n      its top face (the shaft side) between them; the cable leaves the case end below\n      the tabs (photos IMG_5454-5456, indexed in docs/media/photos.md)\n  mounting:\n    note: seated from below (shaft up through the plate), the tab's top face meets\n      the plate, so the rib must be relieved and the case rises tab_top_to_case_top\n      above the plate; seated from above, the tab's underside meets it and the case\n      rises tab_face_to_case_top\n  horns_in_box:\n    note: plastic double-arm, 6-arm star, round disc, 4-arm cross; plus a bought 25T\n      aluminium single arm with a clamped split hub and 2 holes (IMG_5457-5458). aluminium\n      arm measured below (horn_25t_aluminium); plastic horns unmeasured\n  tab_notch:\n    width:\n      measured: 1.51\n    protrudes:\n      measured: 1.8\n      note: Dan confirmed 1.8 mm\n    from_hole_side:\n      measured: 8.31\n      note: '''from the side of the holes to the middle notch'''\n    note: a feature between each tab's two holes that stops the tabs sitting flush\n      (coupon D, round 2)\n  tab_t:\n    measured: 2.28\n  tab_top_to_case_top:\n    measured:\n    - 5.63\n    - 5.69\n    used: 5.69\n    note: 'Dan, with a sketch: from the tab''s top face'\n  tab_face_to_case_top:\n    used: 7.97\n    note: tab top to case top + tab thickness (5.69 + 2.28); the tab's underside sits\n      on the mount\n  horn_25t_aluminium:\n    hub_od:\n      measured: 14.66\n    overall_l:\n      measured: 34.62\n      note: hub's far edge to arm tip\n    hub_t:\n      measured: 5.95\n    arm_t:\n      measured: 3.86\n    end_boss_t:\n      measured: 5.95\n      note: '''on the foot, there''s a stickout'': the raised end around the holes'\n    centre_to_tip:\n      used: 27.29\n      note: overall_l - hub_od / 2\n    hole_centres_from_hub:\n      estimated:\n      - 20.4\n      - 24.1\n      note: from photo IMG_5457 proportions scaled to centre_to_tip; about +-1 mm,\n        not measured\n    hole_d:\n      measured: 2.55\n      note: from the earlier readings ('these little holes')\n    earlier_readings:\n      note: 3.13 (likely the clamp screw hole), 1.52, 4.43, 65.88 (heard as '6.5.88');\n        features unconfirmed\nbearings:\n- id: 608ZZ\n  od:\n    measured: 21.22\n    used: 22.0\n    note: 608 OD is 22.000 -0.008; the video shows the bearing pinched at the jaw\n      tips at an angle, which reads low. Re-measure\n  bore:\n    measured: 7.94\n    nominal: 8.0\n  width:\n    measured: null\n    nominal: 7.0\n```\n\n## How to work\nYou have these tools:\n- measure(item, what): ask the person to measure one of their own things, such as their desk or their phone. They answer with what they measured, or say they can't measure it.\n- submit_design(...): hand in the finished design. This ends the conversation.\n- report_limitation(wanted, blocked_by, why): say you wanted to use something (a technique, a tool, a part or material, a way of handing in the design) but couldn't because of what's available here. It doesn't change anything, but it's noted.\nYou have at most 8 turns. Hand in a design before they run out.\n\n## The design you hand in\nsubmit_design's schema describes every field. The rules that matter:\n- Every dimension that comes from hardware or the real world is a named param, with its source: measured (the person measured it for you), datasheet, catalog, asked (the person told you), guessed, unknown, or design (your own choice).\n- Each part's `code` is Python that defines build(p) and returns a CadQuery Workplane or Shape. `cq` is already imported, and p.<name> is each param's value. Dimensions that come from hardware or the real world must come from params.\n- Model each part in the orientation it's printed in, with z = 0 on the bed.\n- The manual is the steps the person follows, naming the parts and hardware each step uses."
